window.DL_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What shape does (8, 1, 6, 1) + (7, 1, 5) broadcast to?","o":["It raises an error","(8, 7, 6, 1)","(8, 7, 6, 5)","(8, 1, 6, 5)"],"a":2,"w":"Aligned on the right: 1 vs 5 → 5, 6 vs 1 → 6, 1 vs 7 → 7, and 8 against a padded 1 → 8."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Predictions have shape (N, 1) and targets shape (N,). What does ((pred − y) ** 2).mean() compute?","o":["The mean over an (N, N) matrix of every prediction minus every target","The correct mean squared error","A RuntimeError","The mean squared error of the first example only"],"a":0,"w":"It broadcasts to (N, N). Minimising it makes every prediction the mean of y; measured R² fell from 0.98 to 0.00."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does a.t().view(12) fail on a contiguous (3, 4) tensor?","o":["Transposed tensors cannot be reshaped at all","view only works on the GPU","12 is not the right number of elements","The transpose is a non-contiguous view, and no strides can describe a flat 12-vector over that memory order"],"a":3,"w":"Use reshape, which copies when a view is impossible, or call contiguous() first."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Logits have shape (128, 10). Which call gives each example a probability distribution over the 10 classes?","o":["logits.softmax(dim=0)","logits.softmax(dim=1)","logits.softmax(dim=0).t()","logits.sum(dim=1).softmax(dim=0)"],"a":1,"w":"dim names the axis that is normalised over: the classes. Over dim 0 the model in this chapter fell from 84.0% to 36.6%."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tensors, Shapes & Broadcasting","f":"p1-tensors-shapes-and-broadcasting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How much extra memory did expanding a (1, 4096) tensor to (4096, 4096) allocate, against repeat?","o":["64 MB each","32 MB for expand, 64 MB for repeat","0 MB for expand (stride 0), 64 MB for repeat","0 MB for both"],"a":2,"w":"expand is a view with stride 0 along the stretched axis; repeat copies the data 4,096 times."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"X is (64, 784), W is (784, 256) and G = ∂L/∂(XW) is (64, 256). What is ∂L/∂W?","o":["G Wᵀ","Xᵀ G","G Xᵀ","X G"],"a":1,"w":"Xᵀ (784, 64) times G (64, 256) is (784, 256), W’s shape. It also sums the 64 per-example contributions."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does every row of the softmax Jacobian sum to zero?","o":["Softmax is linear","The Jacobian is diagonal","Logits are centred to zero","Probabilities always sum to 1, so moving any logit only moves probability between classes"],"a":3,"w":"Measured for z = (2, 1, 0.1, −1): every row summed to 0 and J matched diag(p) − ppᵀ exactly."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does one backward pass of backprop compute, in Jacobian terms?","o":["A vector-Jacobian product: the loss gradient pushed back through each layer","The full Jacobian of the network","One column of the Jacobian per parameter","The Hessian"],"a":0,"w":"Reverse mode never builds a Jacobian; it multiplies a vector by each layer’s Jacobian implicitly."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A finite-difference gradient check in float32 gives relative error 10⁻³. What should you conclude?","o":["The gradient is certainly correct","The gradient is certainly wrong","Nothing yet: float32 could only reach about 10⁻⁴ here at best, so rerun in float64","Use a smaller h, such as 10⁻⁹"],"a":2,"w":"In float64 the measured error fell to 2.6 × 10⁻¹⁰; at h = 10⁻⁹ in float32 the estimate was exactly 0."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Matrix Calculus: Jacobians & the Chain Rule","f":"p2-matrix-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A bias b of shape (256,) is added to activations of shape (64, 256). What is ∂L/∂b?","o":["The upstream gradient G, shape (64, 256)","G summed over the batch axis, shape (256,)","G averaged over the 256 features","Zero, because biases do not affect the loss"],"a":1,"w":"b was broadcast over the batch in the forward pass, so its gradient sums over the batch in the backward pass."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does bf16 trade away to get fp32’s range in 16 bits?","o":["The sign bit","Exponent bits","Mantissa bits: it keeps only 7, about two significant digits","Nothing; it is strictly better than fp16"],"a":2,"w":"bf16 is 1 sign, 8 exponent and 7 mantissa bits; fp16 is 1, 5 and 10. Its epsilon is 7.8 × 10⁻³ against fp16’s 9.8 × 10⁻⁴."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You add 0.01 to an fp16 accumulator 100,000 times. What do you get?","o":["32: once the gap between fp16 numbers exceeds 0.02, each addition rounds back to the same value","1,000 exactly","inf","NaN"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: it was stuck at 32.0 by step 3,000. bf16 stuck at 4; torch.sum, which accumulates in fp32, gave 1,000."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does subtracting the maximum logit before exponentiating not change the softmax?","o":["It does change it slightly, but the error is small","Because the maximum logit is always zero","Because exp is linear","Because exp(z − m) / Σ exp(z − m) = exp(z) / Σ exp(z): the factor exp(−m) cancels"],"a":3,"w":"Softmax depends only on differences between logits, so the shift is free and keeps every exponent at most 0."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The formula E[x²] − E[x]² returned a variance of −8 in fp32. What happened?","o":["The data had negative values","Catastrophic cancellation: two numbers of about 10⁸, each rounded to the nearest 8, were subtracted to find a difference of 1","fp32 overflowed","The GPU has a bug"],"a":1,"w":"Centring first (what torch.var does) gave 0.9992, matching float64."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Floating Point for Deep Learning","f":"p3-floating-point-for-deep-learning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does loss scaling do in fp16 training?","o":["Clips large gradients","Lowers the learning rate","Multiplies the loss, and so every gradient, by a constant so small gradients stay above fp16’s underflow threshold; it is divided out before the step","Converts the loss to bf16"],"a":2,"w":"Scaling by 1,024 moved all but 0.001% of the measured gradients into fp16’s range. bf16 does not need it."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A module stores its hidden layers as self.layers = [nn.Linear(784, 256), nn.Linear(256, 256)] . What happens?","o":["PyTorch raises an error at construction","The layers run in forward but are not in parameters() or state_dict(), so they never train and are never saved","Everything works normally","The layers train but are not saved"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: 2,570 of 269,322 parameters were visible; accuracy 75.8% vs 87.1%. Use nn.ModuleList."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does loss.backward() do to a parameter’s .grad?","o":["Replaces it with the new gradient","Sets it to zero","Applies the optimiser step","Adds the new gradient to whatever is already there"],"a":3,"w":"Gradients accumulate, which is why zero_grad() is needed each step (and why gradient accumulation works)."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did a forward pass use 896 MB with autograd but 256 MB under torch.no_grad()?","o":["With autograd on, every operation keeps the tensors its backward pass will need","no_grad uses fp16","no_grad skips some layers","no_grad frees the weights"],"a":0,"w":"The arithmetic is identical (both took about 23 ms); only the saved activations differ."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You need to evaluate a model with dropout and batch norm. What do you call?","o":["Only model.eval()","Only torch.no_grad()","Both: model.eval() switches dropout and batch norm to inference behaviour, no_grad stops recording the graph","Neither; PyTorch detects evaluation automatically"],"a":2,"w":"They control different things; evaluation needs both."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"PyTorch Essentials: Modules & Autograd","f":"p4-pytorch-modules-and-autograd.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Calling L.backward() a second time raises “Trying to backward through the graph a second time”. Why?","o":["Gradients can only be computed once per model","The first backward freed the tensors the graph had saved; pass retain_graph=True if you truly need a second pass","The loss became zero","The optimiser has not stepped yet"],"a":1,"w":"Freeing the graph as backward goes is what keeps training memory bounded."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On this Windows machine, why was the first epoch with 8 workers slower than with 0?","o":["Workers compete for the GPU","pin_memory was off","Each spawned worker starts a new Python process and imports PyTorch, about 1.4 s each, before producing a batch","Workers process images in a different order"],"a":2,"w":"From epoch 2, with persistent workers, 8 workers delivered 161,000 img/s against 34,000 with none."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The loader produces 18,000 img/s and the training step consumes 29,600 img/s. What happens?","o":["The GPU waits for data for roughly 40% of each step","The loader drops images to keep up","Training runs at 29,600 img/s","The batch size shrinks automatically"],"a":0,"w":"The slower stage sets the pace: 18,000/29,600 ≈ 61% busy."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Fashion-MNIST sorted by label, trained without shuffling: what did the model predict on the test set?","o":["Every class equally","Only class 0","The correct class 86% of the time","Class 8 or 9, the last classes it saw, for about 98% of images"],"a":3,"w":"Each run of batches pulled the weights towards one class; it ended fitting the end of the file. Accuracy 14.9%."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A medical dataset has several images per patient. How should you split it?","o":["Randomly by image, with a fixed seed","By patient, so all of a patient’s images land in the same split","By image, stratified by label","It does not matter as long as the test set is large"],"a":1,"w":"Otherwise the model can score well by recognising patients rather than reading images."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Datasets, DataLoaders & Batching","f":"p5-datasets-and-dataloaders.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"When does a custom collate_fn become necessary?","o":["Whenever num_workers is above 0","When labels are integers","When examples have different shapes, such as variable-length sequences, so the default stacking fails","Only for iterable-style datasets"],"a":2,"w":"The default collate stacks tensors of equal shape; padding or keeping lists needs your own function."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Minimising mean squared error is maximum likelihood under which assumption?","o":["Targets follow a Laplace distribution around the prediction","Targets are the prediction plus Gaussian noise of constant variance","Targets are categorical","There is no noise"],"a":1,"w":"The Gaussian log-density is −(y − f)²/2σ² plus terms that do not depend on the weights."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A new 10-class classifier’s first-batch loss is 2.31. What does that suggest?","o":["It has already learned a lot","The loss function is broken","It is predicting close to uniform probabilities, as an untrained model should: log 10 ≈ 2.303","The learning rate is too high"],"a":2,"w":"Measured on a fresh MLP: 2.312. A start far from log K points to a bug."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the gradient of cross-entropy with respect to the logits?","o":["softmax(z) − onehot(y)","z − y","−1 / p_y","softmax(z) · (1 − softmax(z))"],"a":0,"w":"Large when the model is confidently wrong, which is why it trains well; squared error on softmax lacks this."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why predict log σ² rather than σ² in a Gaussian NLL output?","o":["It is more accurate","PyTorch requires it","It makes the loss linear","Any real number is a valid log-variance, so the variance stays positive with no constraint"],"a":3,"w":"A raw variance output could reach zero or go negative and make the NLL infinite."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability, Likelihood & Loss Functions","f":"p6-likelihood-and-losses.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which identity links cross-entropy, entropy and KL divergence?","o":["H(p, q) = H(p) − KL(p ‖ q)","H(p, q) = H(p) + KL(p ‖ q)","KL(p ‖ q) = KL(q ‖ p)","H(p, q) = H(q)"],"a":1,"w":"Checked here: 1.4998 = 0.5003 + 0.9995. With one-hot targets H(p) = 0."}];
